 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** Alternatives considered: five-second HTTPS polling — the simplest stateless operation, but repeated reads and seconds of lag; the store would need cursor queries, delivery would stay request/response, and browsers would run a polling loop. A hosted realtime service — the quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection operations, but recurring fees and provider coupling; the store would need publication reconciliation, delivery would wrap the provider, and browsers would use its subscription model. Self-hosted WebSockets were chosen to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor; the accepted cost is owning connection capacity and reconnect problems in delivery, replayable revisions and reliable publication in the store, and reconnect and catch-up logic in browsers. Recorded in `docs/decisions/self-hosted-websockets-delivery.md`.
